    <statement><p>For each of the following, solve exactly for the variable <m>x</m>.</p>
<p><alert>(a)</alert>
<m>1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2!} + \frac{x^3}{3!} + \cdots = 5</m></p>
<p><m>x =</m> <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><alert>(b)</alert>
<m>1 -  x +  x^2 -  x^3 + \cdots = 0.8</m></p>
<p><m>x =</m> <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\ln\mathopen{}\left(5\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{1-0.8}{1\cdot 0.8}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><alert>(a)</alert>
Since <m>1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2!} + \frac{x^3}{3!} + \cdots</m> is the Taylor series for <m>e^x</m>, we know its sum:
<me>1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2!} + \frac{x^3}{3!} + \cdots = e^x = 5.</me>
Solving for <m>x</m>, we get <m>x = \ln(5)</m>.





<alert>(b)</alert>
Similarly, since <m>1 -  x +  x^2 -  x^3 + \cdots</m> is a geometric series with base <m>- x</m>, we know its sum
as well:
<me>1 -  x +  x^2 -  x^3 + \cdots = \frac{1}{1 +  x} = 0.8.</me>
Solving for <m>x</m>, we get <m>x = \frac{1}{4}</m>.



</solution>
